**Reasoning:**

The 'BlueTEC' designation was Mercedes-Benz's brand name for its diesel engines featuring advanced NOx reduction technology, primarily Selective Catalytic Reduction (SCR) using AdBlue (diesel exhaust fluid). This branding was used extensively on previous E-Class generations (W211, W212, W213) equipped with diesel engines.

However, for the *most recent* E-Class generation (W214, launched for the 2024 model year), Mercedes-Benz no longer explicitly uses the 'BlueTEC' badge on its diesel models, although the underlying SCR technology is still employed.

Therefore, to evaluate the "most recent version" of the 'BlueTEC' concept within the E-Class lineup, I will assess the current W214 diesel model, specifically the E 220 d, which is the primary diesel offering in markets like Europe. This model features a 2.0L inline-4 mild-hybrid diesel engine (OM654M) with SCR emission control.

